    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=499260753_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Staff Accountant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nComputes, classifies, records, and verifies numerical data for use in maintaining accurate accounting records. EXPECTATIONS OF EMPLOYEE · Adheres to College Policy and Procedures · Acts as a role model within and outside the College · Performs duties as workload necessitates · Maintains a positive and respectful attitude · Communicates regularly with supervisor about Department issues · Demonstrates flexible and efficient time management and ability to prioritize workload · Consistently reports to work on time prepared to perform duties of position · Meets Department productivity standards ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES include the following. Other duties may be assigned. · High attention to detail and strong problem-solving capabilities · Proficient with excel using VLOOKUP and pivot tables as needed to efficiently analyze data, identify variances, etc. · Reconcile monthly bank statements and resolve any discrepancies · Reconcile monthly general ledger accounts and resolve any discrepancies · Perform data entry of miscellaneous journal entries, including transfers / reclassifications · Support monthly, quarterly, and annual closing cycles by reviewing accounts, etc. · Assist in the preparation of monthly and quarterly financial reports / statements · Assist with the year-end audit with preparation of support schedules, financials, etc. · Set up / maintain on-line access to accounts for all personnel · Prepare sales tax reports · Perform quarterly grant accounting/funds draw downs · Load annual budget files/perform data entry of budget adjustments · Prepare quarterly investment journal entries/schedules · Reviews bank account activity daily and processes direct deposits, ACHs, stop payments, etc. via the bank’s website. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES This job has no supervisory responsibilities. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS & ABILITIES: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. · Education and / or Experience -- Bachelor's degree (B. A.) in Accounting or Finance, from four-year college or university; or one to two years related experience and/or training; or equivalent combination of education and experience. · Communication / Language -- Requires effective oral and written communication skills, excellent interpersonal skills, and intermediate computer literacy. Ability to read, analyze, and interpret common financial reports and legal documents. Ability to write routine reports and correspondence. Ability to respond to common inquiries or complaints from members of the college community and outside vendors. · Mathematical -- Ability to work with mathematical concepts such as probability and statistical inference, and fundamentals of plane and solid geometry and trigonometry. Ability to apply concepts such as fractions, percentages, ratios, and proportions to practical situations. · Reasoning -- Ability to identify problems, collect data, solve practical problems and deal with a variety of concrete variables in situations where only limited standardization exists. Ability to interpret a variety of instructions furnished in written, oral, diagram, or schedule form. · Certificates, Licenses, Registrations -- None required · Computer Literacy / General Office Equipment – Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (Excel. Word, Outlook), CARS / Jenzabar System, Internet. Basic office equipment to include facsimile, copier machines, printer, calculator. PHYSICAL DEMANDS / WORK ENVIRONMENT · Regularly o Sit o Talk or hear · Frequently o Use hands to finger, handle, or feel o Reach with hands and arms · Occasionally o Stand and walk o Stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl o Climb or balance o Lift and/or move up to 25 pounds · Vision o Close vision o Ability to adjust focus · Noise o Moderate · Stress o Low to moderate · Normal Work Schedule o Monday – Friday o 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., with an hour for lunch o Extra hours as necessary to meet the demands of the position",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=609030573_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Computer Scie",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nThe Department of Mathematics, Physics, and Computer Science at Georgetown College invites applications for a Visiting Assistant Professor of Computer Science, beginning in August 2026, pending final funding approval. The successful candidate will hold at least a Master’s degree in an appropriate field by the start date of the position; a PhD is preferred. This position is renewable for up to three years; should a tenure-track line open within that time frame, the successful candidate would be eligible to apply for it. The Department offers B.A. degrees in mathematics and engineering mathematics and minors in computer science, mathematics, physics and data analysis. In addition, the Department hosts a pre-Engineering transfer program and participates in a consortium through which students can obtain majors in web design and information technology (and a cybersecurity major is in the proposal stage). Emphasis is placed on teaching excellence in the context of a church-related college; research and professional development are encouraged and supported. The teaching load is the equivalent of four three-hour courses per semester. The successful candidate would join a team of three mathematicians and two physicists who value active learning in the classroom and the systematic development, both in class and in extracurricular activities, of communication and collaboration skills in addition to the critical thinking and problem-solving skills typically associated with the study of mathematics and related areas. We value—and have always enjoyed—strong collegiality both within the Department and across campus. Students in our Department often participate in either our institution’s Science Honors Program, which funds a summer of undergraduate research, or in its Oxford Honors Program, under which our mathematics majors can spend one or two terms at Oxford University. We currently teach elementary computer programming and data science courses in the department; the successful candidate will be able also to guide students in computational work in advanced courses and in senior Honors projects. The College does not discriminate, either in the admission of students, the hiring and promotion of employees, or in the administration of any of its educational policies, programs, or activities on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, age or disability. Georgetown College is a historically Baptist institution and committed to remaining a broadly Christian institution in the 21st century. The college does not require faculty to be part of any particular denomination or to commit to any particular creedal or faith statement, but just as the college is rooted in Christian love and service, we expect our faculty to actively live out those values as well. The College may use religion as a factor in making faculty employment decisions and in Board of Trustees appointments. Interested persons should submit a cover letter, vita, copies of transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, and three letters of recommendation. Apply at Career Opportunities at Georgetown College Review of applications will begin on November 9, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. More information about the college can be found at www.georgetowncollege.edu",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=607204448_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nGeorgetown College seeks applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor of English, beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will have either a Ph.D. or MFA and will be qualified to teach fiction writing and American literature. The position carries a 4/4 load per academic year, including composition and literature surveys. Editing experience or experience teaching editing and publishing is desirable, since the successful candidate will have the opportunity to teach in our new Editing and Publishing minor. Located in Georgetown, Kentucky, Georgetown College is a private, Christian, liberal arts institution of approximately 1,200 undergraduate students, and emphasis is placed on teaching excellence in a diverse context. It does not discriminate in the admission of students, the hiring and promotion of employees, or in the administration of any of its educational policies, programs, or activities on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, age or disability. Georgetown College is a historically Baptist institution, and all full-time tenure-track faculty are expected to be Christian. However, in keeping with its firm commitment to academic freedom, no creed or statement of faith is imposed upon employees, nor is any one theological orientation or expression of spirituality singled out for special favor. Screening will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To be considered for the position, send letter of application, vita, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, three letters of recommendation, and a fiction writing sample. A critical essay may be included as well.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=605066612_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Bursar",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nThis position’s primary function is to administer the Bursar’s Office affairs of the College. EXPECTATIONS OF EMPLOYEE · Adhere to College Policy and Procedures · Act as a role model within and outside the College · Perform Duties as workload necessitates · Maintain a positive and respectful attitude · Communicate regularly with supervisor about Department issues · Demonstrate flexible and efficient time management and ability to prioritize workload · Consistently report to work on time prepared to perform duties of position · Meet Department productivity standards ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES include the following. Other duties may be assigned. · Analyze and organize office operations and procedures related to student accounting, billing, cash receipts and credit card processing. · Responsible for billing of non-student activities. · Formulate, with the Business Operations VP, Controller and CFO/Treasurer, policies and procedures governing financial relations with students, dormitories, food services, bookstore, Pub/Dup and recreational and parking facilities. · Evaluate procedures throughout the Business Office and identify process improvements that will improve effectiveness and/or internal controls. · Audit financial status of student organization accounts, tuition, housing, meals, etc. · Administer the financial aspects of student loans, scholarships, and student credits. · Oversee collections activities for student accounts. · Record and reconcile donor accounts with development records. · Assist with the planning / organization of New Student Orientation workshops. · Recruit, supervise, oversee training and coordinate activities of staff within department. · Maximize office productivity through proficient use of appropriate software applications. · Perform background duties within the College’s accounting systems, as required. · Oversee Billing/Maintaining Tables SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES Directly supervises all personnel in the Bursar’s Office. Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with the organization's policies and applicable laws. Responsibilities include interviewing, hiring, and training employees; planning, assigning, and directing work; appraising performance; rewarding and disciplining employees; addressing complaints and resolving problems. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS & ABILITIES: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. · Education and / or Experience -- Bachelor's degree (B. A.) from a four-year college or university; or one to two years related experience and/or training; or equivalent combination of education and experience. · Communication / Language -- Requires effective oral and written communication skills, excellent interpersonal skills. Ability to read, analyze, and interpret common scientific and technical journals, financial reports, and legal documents. Ability to respond to common inquiries or complaints from customers, regulatory agencies, or members of the business community. Ability to write speeches and articles for publication that conform to prescribed style and format. Ability to effectively present information to top management, public groups, and/or boards of directors. · Mathematical -- Ability to calculate figures and amounts such as discounts, interest, commissions, proportions, percentages, area, circumference, and volume. Ability to apply concepts of basic algebra and geometry. · Reasoning -- Ability to define problems, collect data, establish facts, and draw valid conclusions. Ability to interpret an extensive variety of technical instructions in mathematical or diagram form and deal with several abstract and concrete variables. · Certificates, Licenses, Registrations -- None Required · Computer Literacy / General Office Equipment -- Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (Excel. Word, Outlook), CARS / Jenzabar System, Internet. Basic office equipment to include facsimile, copier machines, printer. PHYSICAL DEMANDS / WORK ENVIRONMENT · Regularly o Sit o Talk or hear · Frequently o Use hands to finger, handle, or feel o Reach with hands and arms · Occasionally o Stand and walk o Stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl o Climb or balance o Lift and/or move up to 25 pounds o Outside weather conditions · Vision o Close vision o Ability to adjust focus · Noise o Moderate · Stress o Moderate to high · Normal Work Schedule o Monday – Friday o 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., with an hour for lunch o Overtime as necessary to meet the demand s of the position.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=602393541_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nGeorgetown College is seeking a Financial Aid Counselor. The Financial Aid Counselor is a student-centered professional who advises students and their families on financial aid issues, collaborates with colleagues to facilitate student recruitment and retention efforts, awards financial aid in accordance with federal, state and institutional regulations.  EXPECTATIONS OF EMPLOYEE Adhere to College Policy and Procedures Act as a role model within and outside the College Perform duties as workload necessitates Maintain a positive and respectful attitude Communicate regularly with the supervisor about Department issues Demonstrate flexible and efficient time management and ability to prioritize workload Consistently report to work on time prepared to perform duties of the position Meet Department productivity standards ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES  include the following. Other duties may be assigned. Maintain compliance with state and federal regulations as they pertain to student financial planning. Provide guidance on completing and submitting financial aid applications and documents Analyze and evaluate the financial viability of students and families and provide advice and counsel regarding available financial aid opportunities, eligibility requirements, and the application process.  Primary contact for office including phone and email Document management for the office including management of existing files Attend workshops and conferences for professional development Coordinate financial aid activities with Business Office, Registrar’s Office, Admissions Offices, Athletics, Student Success and the Development Office Assists in managing daily operations of the Office of Financial Planning Performs all other duties and responsibilities assigned by the Director of Financial Planning KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS & ABILITIES:  To be successful in this role, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or abilities necessary to perform job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. General knowledge of financial aid processes in higher education environment and of related trends, issues and practices.  Education and / or Experience –  Bachelor’s degree (B.A.) or equivalent preferred  Communication / Language --  Requires effective oral and written communication skills, excellent interpersonal skills, and intermediate computer literacy. Ability to read, analyze, and interpret general business periodicals, professional journals, technical procedures, or governmental regulations. Ability to write reports, business correspondence, and procedure manuals. Ability to effectively present information and respond to questions from groups of managers, clients, customers, and the general public. Mathematical - - Ability to apply advanced mathematical concepts such as exponents, logarithms, quadratic equations, and permutations. Ability to apply mathematical operations to such tasks as frequency distribution, determination of test reliability and validity, analysis of variance, correlation techniques, sampling theory, and factor analysis. Reasoning - - Ability to define problems, collect data, establish facts, and draw reasonable conclusions. Ability to interpret an extensive variety of technical instructions in mathematical or diagram form and deal with several abstract and concrete variables. Certificates, Licenses, Registrations - - None required Computer Literacy / General Office Equipment –  Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (Excel. Word, Outlook), CARS / Jenzabar System, Internet. Basic office equipment to include facsimile, copier machines, printer. PHYSICAL DEMANDS / WORK ENVIRONMENT Regularly Sit Use hands to finger, handle, or feel Reach with hands and arms Talk or hear Lift and/or move up to 10 pounds Occasionally Stand and walk Lift and/or move up to 25 pounds Exposure to outside weather conditions Vision Close vision Distance vision Ability to adjust focus   Noise Moderate Stress Moderate Normal Work Schedule Monday – Friday 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., with an hour for lunch Overtime as necessary to meet the demands of the position",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=602348834_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Clinical Professor of Counselor Education Ope",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nGeorgetown College invites applications for a clinical faculty position in Counselor Education within our new graduate counseling program. The program currently offers a Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC) specialization and is expanding its offerings to include a School Counseling specialization and Addiction Counseling Certificate. We are seeking a faculty member with expertise in Clinical Mental Health Counseling or School Counseling, and competence in Addiction counseling is helpful, who is excited about the opportunity to contribute to the growth and development of a new counselor education program. The successful candidate will contribute to graduate teaching, student advising and mentorship, curriculum development, supervision, scholarship, professional and institutional service, and the program’s continued development and pursuit of CACREP accreditation. The program is delivered entirely online through a combination of synchronous and asynchronous learning experiences. An in-person faculty presence is preferred; however, highly qualified candidates who can effectively contribute to a fully online program may be considered for a remote appointment. Essential Qualifications: · Earned Ph.D. in Counselor Education and Supervision from a CACREP-accredited program. · Experience teaching graduate-level counseling courses. · Experience with CACREP accreditation and/or CACREP standards. · Eligibility for or possession of appropriate professional credentials related to counseling. · Demonstrated ability to teach effectively in a fully online environment using synchronous and asynchronous instructional methods. · Commitment to counselor professional identity, ethical practice, multicultural competence, and evidence-based counseling practice. · Demonstrated commitment to graduate student advising, mentorship, and professional development. · Evidence of scholarly activity appropriate to the candidate’s academic rank. Responsibilities The successful candidate will: · Teach courses aligned with the faculty member’s area of specialization, while contributing to the broader curriculum as appropriate. · Develop and deliver engaging online instruction through synchronous and asynchronous formats. · Advise and mentor graduate counseling students. · Support students through practicum, internship, and professional development experiences. · Contribute to curriculum development and continuous program improvement. · Participate in CACREP-related planning, assessment, documentation, and accreditation activities. · Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the candidate’s academic rank. · Participate in college, program, and professional service. · Support student recruitment and retention efforts. · Develop and maintain relationships with relevant community and professional partners. · Collaborate with faculty colleagues to support the continued growth and development of the Counselor Education program. Georgetown College is a private, Christian, liberal arts institution and emphasis is placed on teaching excellence in a diverse context. It is located in the small college town of Georgetown, Kentucky, just 12 miles north of Lexington. Georgetown College is developing a new, fully online Counselor Education program designed to prepare ethical, competent, and culturally responsive professional counselors. The program currently offers a Clinical Mental Health Counseling specialization and is expanding its offerings to include School Counseling. The program incorporates both synchronous and asynchronous instruction, providing students with the flexibility of online education while maintaining meaningful opportunities for faculty engagement and professional development. As a growing program, faculty will have an important opportunity to help shape curriculum, develop the School Counseling specialization, establish community partnerships, and contribute to the program’s pursuit of CACREP accreditation. Georgetown College does not discriminate, either in the admission of students, the hiring and promotion of employees, or in the administration of any of its educational policies, programs, or activities on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, age or disability. Georgetown College is a historically Baptist institution and committed to remaining a broadly Christian institution in the 21st century. The college does not require faculty to be part of any particular denomination or to commit to any particular creedal or faith statement, but just as the college is rooted in Christian love and service, we expect our faculty to actively live out those values as well. The College may use religion as a factor in making faculty employment decisions and in Board of Trustees appointments. Screening will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To be considered for the position, upload letter of application, vita, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, and three letters of recommendation.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=602137807_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "School Counseling",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nGeorgetown College invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in School Counseling within our new Counselor Education master’s program. We are seeking a faculty member with expertise in School Counseling who is excited about the opportunity to help develop and launch the School Counseling program at GC. The successful candidate will contribute to graduate teaching, student advising and mentorship, curriculum development, clinical supervision, scholarship, professional and institutional service, and the continued development of the Counselor Education program and its pursuit of CACREP Accreditation. The program is delivered entirely online through a combination of synchronous and asynchronous learning experiences. An in-person faculty presence is preferred; however, highly qualified candidates who can effectively contribute to a fully online program may be considered for a remote appointment. Essential Qualifications: · Earned Ph.D. in Counselor Education and Supervision from a CACREP-accredited program. · Demonstrated expertise/specialization in School counseling. · Experience with CACREP accreditation. · Experience teaching graduate level counseling courses. · Demonstrated ability to teach in a fully online environment using synchronous and asynchronous instructional methods. · Eligibility for or possession of appropriate professional credentials related to counseling/and or school counseling. · Demonstrated commitment to graduate student advising, mentorship, and professional development. · Evidence of scholarly activity. Responsibilities: · Teach courses primarily related to School Counseling, while contributing to the broader Counselor Education curriculum as appropriate. · Contribute to the development and implementation of the new School Counseling specialization. · Develop and deliver engaging online instruction through synchronous and asynchronous formats. · Advise and mentor graduate counseling students. · Support students through practicum, internship, and professional development experiences. · Contribute to curriculum development and continuous program improvement. · Participate in CACREP-related planning, assessment, documentation, and accreditation activities. · Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the candidate’s academic rank. · Participate in college, program, and professional service. · Support student recruitment and retention efforts. · Develop and maintain relationships with schools, school districts, professional organizations, and other community partners. · Collaborate with faculty colleagues to support the continued development and growth of the Counselor Education program. Georgetown College is a private, Christian, liberal arts institution and emphasis is placed on teaching excellence in a diverse context. It is located in the small college town of Georgetown, Kentucky, just 12 miles north of Lexington. Georgetown College is developing a new, fully online Counselor Education program designed to prepare ethical, competent, and culturally responsive professional counselors. The program currently offers a Clinical Mental Health Counseling specialization and is expanding its offerings to include School Counseling. The program incorporates both synchronous and asynchronous instruction, providing students with the flexibility of online education while maintaining meaningful opportunities for faculty engagement and professional development. As a growing program, faculty will have an important opportunity to help shape curriculum, develop the School Counseling specialization, establish community partnerships, and contribute to the program’s pursuit of CACREP accreditation. Georgetown College does not discriminate, either in the admission of students, the hiring and promotion of employees, or in the administration of any of its educational policies, programs, or activities on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, age or disability. Georgetown College is a historically Baptist institution and committed to remaining a broadly Christian institution in the 21st century. The college does not require faculty to be part of any particular denomination or to commit to any particular creedal or faith statement, but just as the college is rooted in Christian love and service, we expect our faculty to actively live out those values as well. The College may use religion as a factor in making faculty employment decisions and in Board of Trustees appointments. Screening will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To be considered for the position, upload letter of application, vita, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, and three letters of recommendation.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.cloud.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d789d92f-3d30-464b-8c76-1fe13a4928bb&ccId=326615217_3&jobId=581795550_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Dean of Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\n\nLocation: Georgetown\n\nGeorgetown College invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track, 12-month position as the Dean of Nursing to develop a new RN-BSN program. We expect to enroll students in this new program beginning in Fall 2027. The Dean of Nursing is responsible for designing a CCNE-aligned curriculum, accreditation leadership, faculty recruitment, practicum/internship partnership development, and teaching. This position reports to the Provost and includes administrative and instructional duties. Qualifications · Graduate degree in nursing · Active RN license or eligibility for licensure in the state of KY · Leadership experience in Nursing Education · Accreditation leadership and program development experience is preferred · Curriculum development and online teaching experience is preferred Georgetown College Georgetown College is a private, Christian, liberal arts institution serving approximately 1100 undergraduate students, located in Georgetown, Kentucky, just 12 miles north of Lexington. The College is a historically Baptist institution, and all full-time tenure-track faculty are expected to be Christian; in keeping, however, with Georgetown’s firm commitment to academic freedom, no creed or statement of faith is imposed upon employees, nor is any one theological orientation or expression of spirituality singled out for special favor. The College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, age or disability.  The College may use religion as a factor in making faculty employment decisions and in Board of Trustees appointments.  Review of applications will begin on October 1 and will continue until the position is filled. To be considered for the position, submit a curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, description of leadership experience, and the names of three references who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
